first 300: The Seasonal Almanac\n\nby Dorothy Whitfield\n\nChapter 1: Spring\n\nSpring arrives not as a single event but as a sequence of small awakenings. The soil loosens before the first shoots appear. Birdsong returns before the leaves do. The careful observer notices these precursors and prepares accordingly. T
last 300: nd planting window opens for cool-weather crops that will carry through early winter.\n\nPreserving the Harvest\n\nCanning, drying, and root-cellaring each suit different produce. Tomatoes and fruits preserve well in jars. Herbs dry readily in bundled bunches hung in a warm, dry space with good airflow.
